Thanks — I can help. First, a quick check: one of the numbers you gave is inconsistent with the others, so I’ll show the math and then give narrative text you can use depending on which burn definition is correct.

Quick facts you provided
- Cash: $5.2M
- Runway stated: 20 months
- Burn rate stated: $72K / month
- Revenue last quarter: $670K

Inconsistency to resolve
- If net burn = $72K/month, runway = 5.2M ÷ 72K ≈ 72 months (about 6 years), not 20 months.
- If runway = 20 months, implied net burn = 5.2M ÷ 20 = $260K/month (i.e., cash used per month).
Please confirm whether the $72K is:
- net burn (cash outflow after revenue), or
- gross burn (total cash operating costs), or
- something else (e.g., weekly number, or excluding certain items).  
I’ll provide narratives for both likely interpretations below.

Core calculations (useful numbers)
- Monthly revenue (last quarter): 670K ÷ 3 ≈ $223K/month
- Annualized revenue (run-rate): 670K × 4 = $2.68M ARR

Scenario A — if net burn = $72K / month
- Runway = 5,200,000 ÷ 72,000 ≈ 72.2 months
- Net cash flow per month = -$72K (cash outflow)
- With revenue ~$223K/month, that implies total monthly cash outflow (operating spend) ≈ 295K/month (because 295K expenses − 223K revenue = 72K net burn)
Narrative summary (Scenario A, positive framing)
- “As of today we hold $5.2M in cash and are generating roughly $223K/month (Q-trend). Our current net cash burn is about $72K/month, giving us roughly six years of runway (~72 months) at current operating levels. The burn is modest relative to revenue and allows us to invest in growth initiatives without near-term financing pressure. Key drivers of monthly cash flow are [list top expense categories].”
Risks & actions (Scenario A)
- Monitor revenue seasonality — small declines could widen burn.
- Continue to prioritize high-ROI growth investments; consider opportunistic hiring and one-time capital investments.
- Maintain a 12–18 month forecast update cadence.

Scenario B — if runway = 20 months (implied net burn ≈ $260K / month)
- Implied net burn = 5,200,000 ÷ 20 = $260K/month
- With revenue ~$223K/month, that implies total monthly operating spend ≈ $483K/month (because 483K expenses − 223K revenue = 260K net burn)
Narrative summary (Scenario B, cautious framing)
- “We currently hold $5.2M in cash and generated $670K last quarter (≈$223K/month). Based on current spending, our net cash burn is roughly $260K/month, which implies a runway of ~20 months. While revenue covers a meaningful portion of our operating cost, we are still using cash to scale operations. This gives us a moderate runway but requires discipline to ensure we hit revenue or margin milestones before we approach funding decisions.”
Risks & actions (Scenario B)
- Prioritize initiatives that increase revenue or improve gross margin (pricing, commercial expansion, upsell).
- Implement near-term cost controls on discretionary spend to extend runway, aiming for a 25–35% reduction in non-critical costs if needed.
- Prepare fundraising materials and a 12–18 month forecast if fundraising is likely within the next 12 months.

Suggested 1–page finance narrative (pick & adapt the version that matches your numbers)
- Executive summary: “Metro Manufacturing holds $5.2M in cash. Last quarter revenue was $670K (≈$223K/month), and current net burn is [insert confirmed burn]. At that burn rate, runway is approximately [insert months].”
- Drivers: “Revenue growth is driven by [channels/products]; primary outflows are payroll, COGS, inventory/capex, and facility costs. Recent one-time items: [list if applicable].”
