These three cannot all be true at once:
- If net burn = $72k/mo and revenue = $223k/mo, you are cash-positive on an operating basis (expenses ≈ revenue + 72k ≈ $295k/mo), and runway is ~72 months, not 20.
- If runway = 20 months, implied net burn is $260k/mo; with revenue at $223k/mo, that would imply total monthly cash outflows ≈ $483k (i.e., expenses >> revenue), and net burn would be $260k, not $72k.

Before I draft final narrative, please confirm which of the following is correct (or give the definitions/extra numbers):
1) Is the $72k/month the net burn (cash outflow after accounting for revenue) or gross cash outflow (total expenses)?
2) Is the 20 months runway a target/planned runway (e.g., after a planned raise) or the CURRENT computed runway?
3) Do you have recent monthly P&L (last 3–6 months) so we can show trend instead of a flat-quarter snapshot?

