Suggested finance narratives (pick the one that matches your definitions)

A. If $72k is the NET burn (accurate net cash burn today)
Executive summary
- As of today Metro Manufacturing holds $5.2M in cash and is burning about $72k per month net. That gives us an actual runway of ~72 months (6 years).
- Last quarter revenue was $670k (≈ $223k/month), annualized run-rate ≈ $2.68M.
Interpretation & message
- We are in a strong cash position with long runway. This allows us to prioritize strategic investments (R&D, sales expansion, capex for production scaling) and be selective on fundraising timing.
- Recommendations: prepare a 24–36 month plan for planned investments and expected inflection points; model upside scenarios where incremental sales hires or marketing spend accelerate growth and still keep runway comfortable.
Risk / caveat
- A long runway is great, but validate any one‑time items and ensure forecasts incorporate potential revenue seasonality, customer concentration, and capital expenditures.

B. If runway = 20 months (current computed runway) — implied net burn $260k/month
Executive summary
- Metro Manufacturing has $5.2M cash and a current runway of ~20 months, implying monthly net cash burn of approximately $260k.
- Last quarter revenue was $670k (≈ $223k/month), so current monthly operating expenses materially exceed revenue (expenses ≈ $483k/month).
Interpretation & message
- Runway is moderate — gives time to execute on growth and margin improvements but requires near-term actions to avoid pressure within ~18–20 months.
- Priority actions: reduce discretionary spend, accelerate revenue (sales focus, top customer expansion), and prepare a fundraising plan (start outreach now if you want >6–9 months lead time).
Risk / caveat
- This runway implies relatively high operating leverage; you should stress-test against slower revenue growth and any one-off capex or seasonality.

C. If $72k is a partial metric (e.g., payroll-only or non-GAAP metric)
Executive summary
- Clarify which burn is being reported. If $72k excludes key items (capex, one-time spend, debt service), present both “adjusted” and “true” net burn so leadership has an apples-to-apples runway.
Recommendation
- Reconcile GAAP cash outflows (bank cash-change burn) with non-GAAP operating burn and present both in internal materials.

Suggested graphs and KPIs to include (internal materials)
- Single slide “Cash & Runway”:
  - Cash balance (current), implied runway (using net burn), and sensitivity table (runway at net burn, net burn ±25%, and at planned investment cases).
- “Burn vs Revenue” stacked area chart (monthly for last 12 months): revenue on top, expenses broken into major buckets (COGS, R&D, SG&A), net burn line.
- Monthly P&L summary (last 6–12 months) and 12-month cash flow forecast (best / base / downside scenarios).
- KPIs: cash, net burn (monthly), gross burn (monthly), revenue run-rate, gross margin, months of cash (runway), customer concentration (% revenue from top 5 customers).

Recommended wording for an internal slide (pick matching scenario)
- Strong runway example (if net burn = $72k):
  “As of [date], Metro Manufacturing holds $5.2M in cash and is burning ~$72k/month net, supporting ~72 months of runway. Last quarter revenue was $670k (annualized run-rate $2.68M). With this runway we will prioritize selective growth investments while monitoring customer concentration and near-term capital needs.”
- Constrained runway example (if runway = 20 months):
  “As of [date], Metro Manufacturing holds $5.2M in cash, giving us ~20 months of runway under current operating plans (implied net burn ≈ $260k/month). Last quarter revenue was $670k. We will focus on revenue acceleration and cost optimization, and begin investor outreach within the next 3–6 months to secure optionality.”

Recommended next steps / action items
- Reconcile and confirm burn definition (net vs gross) and any one-time items.
- Produce a 12–18 month cash flow model with three scenarios (base, aggressive growth, downside stress).
- Prepare a sensitivity table: runway at net burn, net burn ±25%, and with one-time capex.
- Draft investor materials or internal memo depending on target (raise vs cost optimization).
- Present clear asks to leadership (hire freeze, marketing investment, fundraising timeline).

Would you like me to:
- A) Reconcile the numbers for you if you provide the last 3–6 months of cash flow or P&L?
- B) Draft the single-slide “Cash & Runway” and a one-paragraph narrative for leadership using the scenario you tell me to assume (pick A: net burn = $72k; B: runway = 20 months; or C: provide corrected figures)?
- C) Build the 12-month cash flow scenarios (base / upside / downside) with assumptions?

Tell me which you want next and confirm how you define “burn” (net vs gross) and whether the 20 months is current or target. I can draft the exact slide narrative and the sensitivity table after that.

