- Working capital: Inventory, receivables, and payable timing can materially affect month-to-month cash flows — highlight any concentrations.

Burn trend interpretation
- Current net burn is modest relative to revenue: net burn / monthly revenue ≈ 32% (72k / 223k). That ratio indicates we are cash-generating close to break-even if revenue grows modestly.
- If revenue is steady or growing, current burn suggests runway is ample for product investment or measured expansion. If revenue declines or one-time costs recur, runway could compress quickly — monitor 3–6 month rolling burn.

Scenarios to present to leadership (recommended)
- Base case (keep current pace): Net burn = $72K/month → runway ≈ 72 months. Use this to justify 12–24 month growth investments.
- Conservative case (slower revenue, some hiring/capex): Net burn rises to, e.g., $200–260K/month → runway becomes 26–20 months. This is the case to model if you expect a slowdown or plan heavy near-term investment.
- Upside case (revenue growth + cost optimization): Net burn turns neutral or positive (net cash flow ≥ 0) — demonstrates path to self-funded growth.

