- Metro Manufacturing has a strong liquidity position with $5.2M in cash and a modest net cash burn of ~$72K/month, supporting roughly 72 months of runway at current operations. Last quarter revenue was $670K (≈$223K/month), demonstrating a healthy top-line relative to cash usage.

Detailed narrative (recommended slide/notes)
- Cash & runway: As of today we hold $5.2M in cash. At a net cash burn of $72K/month, our current runway is approximately 72 months (5.2M / 72K). This provides significant flexibility for strategic investments, product development, and measured growth initiatives.
- Revenue context: Last quarter revenue totaled $670K (≈$223K/month). With revenue contributing to cash inflows, our gross monthly operating spend is roughly $295K (revenue + net burn), indicating room to scale marketing or accelerate production while remaining well-capitalized.
- Trend considerations: While runway appears long, we should monitor hiring plans, planned capital expenditures, inventory build, and one-time investments that could materially shorten runway if executed aggressively. Also track seasonal revenue patterns and any customer payment timing risks.
- Action items: Maintain a rolling 12-month cash forecast, establish predefined spend triggers (e.g., hiring or capex only if cash > X or revenue growth Y%), and present a 3-scenario forecast (base / conservative / aggressive) for the exec team.

Narrative B — If runway = 20 months (implied net burn ≈ $260K/month)
Executive summary (short)
- With $5.2M in cash and an internal runway estimate of 20 months, Metro Manufacturing is projecting an implied net cash burn of ~ $260K/month. Last quarter revenue was $670K (≈$223K/month), so runway reflects a more aggressive spend profile or conservatism in projections.

Detailed narrative (recommended slide/notes)
- Cash & runway: Cash on hand is $5.2M, and our internal runway estimate is 20 months. That implies an expected net burn of about $260K/month (5.2M / 20). This is materially higher than our reported cash-basis burn of $72K/month and suggests planned near-term investments or adjustments are included in the runway calculation.
- Revenue context: Last quarter revenue of $670K (~$223K/month) offsets some spending, but if net cash burn is $260K/month this indicates monthly outflows near $483K (spend ≈ revenue + net burn). The larger outflow likely reflects ramped hiring, inventory purchases, capex, or customer acquisition investments.
- Trend considerations: The 20-month runway may intentionally factor in planned scale-up costs or conservative assumptions (e.g., lower revenue growth, extended receivable days). It’s important we surface those assumptions so stakeholders understand whether runway compression comes from growth investments or negative demand trends.
- Action items: Detail the drivers of the higher burn (hiring plan, inventory, one-off costs), run sensitivity scenarios (what happens to runway if revenue declines 10–20%, or if hiring is delayed by 3 months), and identify cost-savings levers or non-dilutive funding options.

Suggested visuals & KPIs for the slide/deck
- One-line summary at top: Cash, runway (months), net burn (monthly), last quarter revenue.
- Chart: 12-month rolling cash balance projection under three scenarios (base / conservative / aggressive).
- Chart: Monthly net burn trend for the last 6–12 months (bars) with a 3-month moving average line.
- Table: Key assumptions (revenue growth rates, hiring/cost plans, one-time items).
- KPIs: Gross margin, revenue/month, cash burn/month, months of runway, customer receivable days, inventory days.

Suggested language for Q&A or board talking points
- “Our cash position is healthy at $5.2M. Depending on whether you use the current net burn of $72K/month or the internally modeled spend profile (which implies ~ $260K/month), runway is approximately 72 months vs. 20 months. We’ve prepared scenario forecasts to show the impact of hiring and capex decisions on runway.”
- “If the board prefers to maintain a minimum runway cushion (e.g., 18 months), we recommend pausing discretionary hires and staging inventory purchases until revenue momentum strengthens, or pursuing a modest bridge round if we accelerate the product roadmap.”

Next steps I can do for you
- If you confirm which figures/definitions are correct, I can draft a one-page cash-flow narrative and a 12-month cash forecast (base/conservative/aggressive).
- If you provide last 6–12 months of actual monthly cash flows (or P&L), I can create a burn trend and visuals to include in slides.
- I can also draft the slide text and bullet points ready to paste into a board deck.

Which version reflects reality (A: net burn = $72K/month, runway ≈72 months; B: runway is intended to be 20 months with implied burn ≈$260K/month), or is there another definition for “burn” you’re using? Provide that and I’ll draft the exact narrative and forecasts.

