- We have $5.2M in cash. On our current reported net cash outflow of $72K/month (expenses net of revenue), that provides approximately 72 months of runway. However, leadership has cited a 20‑month runway, which implies an effective monthly cash outflow of about $260K — this difference is driven by planned incremental spending (hiring, capex, program investments) or an alternate burn definition. We should explicitly state which definition we use in materials and show both scenarios.
- Revenue last quarter was $670K (≈ $223K/month). At current operating levels (monthly expenses ≈ $295K if net burn is $72K), revenue covers most of recurring costs; the primary risk to runway is planned incremental spend or a material revenue decline.

Suggested detailed narrative (for a slide or memo)
- Cash & runway: “As of [date] Metro Manufacturing holds $5.2M in cash. Using the reported net burn of $72K/month (defined as cash outflows minus cash inflows), runway is ~72 months. If we model the 20‑month runway referenced in some planning materials, that reflects an expected increase in monthly net cash outflows to ~$260K/month (due to planned hires, capex, or other initiatives). We recommend labeling both ‘current run-rate runway’ and ‘post-investment runway’ in the deck so stakeholders understand the distinction.”
- Burn trend & drivers: “Current reported net burn of $72K/month implies monthly operating expenses of roughly $295K (since 223K revenue + 72K net burn = 295K expenses). Key drivers of cash consumption are [COGS / manufacturing costs], SG&A (including payroll), and working capital. Planned investment items (hiring a new production team, increase in inventory, one-time tooling or IT spend) explain higher projected burn in the 20‑month scenario.”
- Risks & sensitivities: “A 20% revenue decline (to ~ $179K/month) would increase net burn materially if expenses remain fixed: under current expense assumptions, net burn would rise to ≈ $117K/month, reducing runway to ~45 months. If expenses increase to the $483K/month level (implied by the 20‑month runway), a 20% revenue drop yields a runway closer to ~17 months. These sensitivities should be surfaced to show how hiring or investment choices affect runway.”

Key numbers & sensitivity table (use in a slide)
- Inputs:
  - Cash: $5.2M
  - Revenue (monthly): $223,333
  - Reported net burn: $72K/month → Expenses ≈ $295,333/month
- Calculated:
  - Runway @ net burn $72K = 5,200,000 / 72,000 ≈ 72 months
  - Runway @ implied burn $260K = 5,200,000 / 260,000 = 20 months
- Sensitivity (assuming expenses = $295,333/month):
  - Revenue -20% → net burn ≈ $116,667 → runway ≈ 44.6 months
  - Revenue +20% → net burn ≈ $27,333 → runway ≈ 190 months
- Sensitivity (assuming expenses = $483,333/month — implied by 20‑month runway):
  - Revenue -20% → net burn ≈ $304,666 → runway ≈ 17.1 months
  - Revenue +20% → net burn ≈ $215,333 → runway ≈ 24.1 months

Recommended visuals
- Single-page dashboard: cash balance, runway (two lines: current run-rate and post-investment run-rate), last quarter revenue, current net burn.
- Runway sensitivity chart: x-axis = months of runway; multiple lines for different expense assumptions (+/- hiring or program spend) and revenue outcomes (-20% / base / +20%).
- Monthly trend chart (last 12 months): revenue vs gross burn vs net burn (bars for cash inflows/outflows; line for cumulative cash).
- Waterfall: starting cash → operating cash flow this quarter → major one-time uses (capex, investments) → ending cash.

Suggested recommendations / ask to leadership
- Explicitly define burn (gross vs net) in all communications.
- Approve a 3-scenario reforecast (base, conservative, aggressive) that captures: (a) current run-rate, (b) planned investments (the 20‑month case), (c) downside revenue shocks.
- Delay non-critical hires or projects until the post-investment runway is confirmed, or fund them from a separately approved project budget.
- Provide monthly P&L and cash flow for the last 12 months so we can produce the burn trend chart and a one-page deck.

One-paragraph executive soundbite you can use
- “We hold $5.2M in cash. At our current reported net burn of $72K/month (expenses after revenue), runway is approximately 6 years. If we proceed with planned investments that increase monthly outflows to the level used in our 20‑month estimate, runway contracts to ~20 months. We will present both the current-run-rate and post-investment forecasts with sensitivity to +/-20% revenue to ensure leadership understands the cash implications of investment timing.”

