Thanks — I can help. First, one important clarification before I draft final wording: the numbers you gave are inconsistent as stated. Quick check:

- Cash: $5.2M
- Burn rate: $72K/month
- Runway: 20 months
- Revenue last quarter: $670K (≈ $223K/month)

If net burn really is $72K/month, runway = 5,200,000 / 72,000 ≈ 72.2 months (~6.0 years), not 20 months. If runway is 20 months, implied net burn = 5,200,000 / 20 = $260K/month (which is very different from $72K). Also note monthly revenue ≈ $223K — whether the company is net-burning less or more than revenue changes the story.

Please confirm:
- Is the $72K figure gross burn (total OPEX) or net burn (OPEX − revenue)?
- Is the 20-month runway a current, confirmed figure? If so, we should use implied net burn = $260K/month.
Once you confirm which numbers are authoritative, I’ll produce a final narrative. Below I give both interpretations, calculations, scenario sensitivity, and suggested narrative text you can drop into internal materials.

1) Quick calculations (both interpretations)
- Monthly revenue = $670K / 3 ≈ $223K/month.
- If net burn = $72K/month:
  - Runway = $5,200,000 / $72,000 ≈ 72.2 months (~6.0 years).
  - Monthly operating expenses = revenue + net burn ≈ $223K + $72K = $295K.
  - Net burn as % of revenue ≈ 32%.
- If runway = 20 months (implied net burn):
  - Implied net burn = $5,200,000 / 20 = $260K/month.
  - Monthly operating expenses = revenue + net burn ≈ $223K + $260K = $483K.
  - Net burn as % of revenue ≈ 117% (i.e., burning more cash than you bring in monthly).

2) Example sensitivity scenarios (illustrates how runway changes)
I show two baseline cases (A = net burn $72K; B = net burn $260K). Assumptions for scenarios: downside = revenue −20% and expenses +10%; upside = revenue +20% and expenses unchanged.

- Case A (net burn = $72K)
  - Base runway: ~72.2 months.
  - Downside runway: ~35.6 months (~3.0 years).
  - Upside runway: ~190 months (~15.9 years).

- Case B (net burn = $260K)
  - Base runway: 20 months.
  - Downside runway: ~14.7 months.
  - Upside runway: ~24.1 months.

Takeaway: runway is highly sensitive to relatively modest changes in revenue or expense run rate — which is why a clear, consistent definition and up-to-date monthly P&L/ cash model is essential.

3) Suggested structure and phrasing for the internal finance narrative
Use short, factual statements with assumptions clearly called out. Sample language you can paste into a slide or memo — adapt after you confirm the burn/runway figure.

Executive summary (2–3 sentences)
- Option A (if net burn = $72K): “As of [date], Metro Manufacturing holds $5.2M in cash. With a net cash burn of ~$72K/month, we have ~72 months of runway under current operations. Revenue for the last quarter was $670K (~$223K/month).”
- Option B (if runway = 20 months / net burn ≈ $260K): “As of [date], Metro Manufacturing holds $5.2M in cash. Given our current net cash burn of ~ $260K/month, we have ~20 months of runway. Revenue for the last quarter was $670K (~$223K/month).”

Current trends / drivers (bullet points)
- Revenue: last quarter $670K — highlight whether QoQ trend is up/flat/down.
- Expense drivers: hiring, manufacturing ramp, inventory purchases, one-time P&L items (list specifics).
- Working capital: AP/AR/inventory days — note any constraints.
- Capex or committed spend in next 12 months that will materially change runway.

Risk & sensitivity summary (one paragraph)
