Thanks — I can help. First, there’s a clear mismatch in the numbers you provided, so I’ll reconcile those, show the math, give a clean executive-summary narrative you can drop into internal materials, and recommend charts/KPIs and next steps. Quick question after this: is the $72K/month figure your “net cash burn” (cash outflows minus operating cash inflows) or is it an “adjusted” (non‑GAAP) burn figure (e.g., excluding one‑time items, financing, or non‑cash charges)? Also: what date is the $5.2M cash balance as of?

1) Key inputs and immediate math
- Cash on hand: $5,200,000
- Reported runway: 20 months (you supplied)
- Reported burn rate: $72,000 / month (you supplied)
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → average monthly revenue = 670,000 / 3 = 223,333 / month

2) Reconciliation and important observations
- Runway = Cash / (monthly net cash burn). If runway = 20 months, implied monthly net cash burn = 5,200,000 / 20 = 260,000 / month.
- That 260K/month implied net burn is NOT the same as the $72K/month you quoted. So one of these is reported using a different definition:
  - If $72K is the net cash burn, then runway would be 5,200,000 / 72,000 ≈ 72.2 months (≈ 6 years), not 20 months.
  - If 20 months is correct, net cash burn must be ~260K/month. With average monthly revenue of ~223K, that implies total cash outflows (expenses + capex + other cash uses) ≈ 223K + 260K = 483K/month.
- Likely causes of the discrepancy:
  - $72K might be an “adjusted” or “operating” burn figure that excludes large cash items (e.g., capex, debt service, tax payments), or excludes non‑cash charges (depreciation, stock comp) or includes a near‑term one‑time cash inflow/outflow smoothing.
  - 20 months runway is probably computed using a different burn definition (full cash burn including planned investments or one‑time items).

3) Clear definitions to include in your materials (use these verbatim so readers are aligned)
- Gross cash burn: total cash operating outflows per month (payroll, rent, suppliers, variable costs, cash taxes).
- Cash inflows: cash receipts from customers (collections of revenue), other operating cash.
- Net cash burn (or runway burn): Gross cash burn − cash inflows (i.e., net reduction in cash each month). This is the number used for runway = cash / net cash burn.
- Adjusted burn: any management-adjusted metric that excludes one‑offs or non‑cash items — must be labeled and reconciled to cash burn.

4) Executive summary narrative (short)
"As of [date], Metro Manufacturing holds $5.2M in cash. Using our current cash-flow assumptions, our runway is between ~11 and ~72 months depending on the burn metric used:
- If we use our reported adjusted burn of $72K/month, we have very healthy runway (~72 months).
- If we use the full cash-basis net burn implied by the 20-month runway, our net cash burn is ~260K/month (this implies monthly cash outflows of roughly $483K against monthly revenue of ~$223K).
We should explicitly state which burn definition we are using in investor/internal materials. I recommend presenting both (adjusted and cash) plus a sensitivity table, and documenting the assumptions (no new financing, revenue flat, and any one‑time items)."

5) Sensitivity table to include (example values)
Compute runway = cash / monthly burn
- Burn = $72K → runway ≈ 72.2 months
- Burn = $150K → runway ≈ 34.7 months
- Burn = $260K → runway = 20.0 months (matches reported runway)
- Burn = $350K → runway ≈ 14.9 months
- Burn = $483K → runway ≈ 10.8 months

(Include these as a small table or chart in the deck so stakeholders can see impact of small changes.)

6) Suggested charts for your internal slide deck
- Cash runway chart: projected cash balance over time under 3 scenarios (best = $72K burn, base = $260K burn, downside = $483K burn).
- Monthly P&L trend: revenue, cash operating expenses, and net cash burn by month for last 6–12 months.
- Revenue vs cash expenses waterfall: show how revenue absorption changes net cash burn.
- Sensitivity chart: months of runway vs monthly net burn.
- Key KPI panel: cash, runway (explicit definition), trailing 3-month revenue, gross margin, monthly net burn, burn trend quarter-over-quarter.

7) Suggested talking points / CFO note (2–3 lines)
- “We currently hold $5.2M in cash. Using our conservative cash-basis net burn of $260K/month, we have 20 months of runway. On an adjusted operating basis (excl. one-time items), our burn is $72K/month — we show both metrics and their assumptions below to provide transparency into cash management decisions.”
- Follow-up: “Major drivers of the current cash outflow are [list top 2–3 items: e.g., increased inventory purchasing, ramped hiring in manufacturing, one-time capex]. If those persist, runway will align with the 20‑month calculation.”

8) Action items / recommended next steps
- Confirm definitions: Which burn metric should be presented as the headline? Add reconciliation slide showing adjustments from GAAP cash decrease to adjusted burn.
- Pull monthly cash receipts and cash disbursements for last 6 months to show trend and validate 72K vs 260K.
- Create the three-scenario cash projection (base, conservative, optimistic) and include triggers for corrective actions (hiring freeze, capex deferral, price increases).
- If you’d like, I can draft the reconciliation paragraph or the CFO note and create the sensitivity table and chart text.

