If you don’t have a preference, I’ll assume $72K is net burn (cash outflow after revenue) and show both net-runway and the more conservative “runway vs gross operating spend” so you can pick the message you want to use.

Below I give: 1) the quick math and reconciliations, 2) a concise cash-flow / burn-trend narrative you can drop into internal materials, 3) suggested slides/charts and recommended next steps.

1) Quick math & reconciliation (using the numbers you provided)
- Cash on hand: $5,200,000
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → monthly average revenue = 670,000 / 3 = $223,333
- If $72,000 is net cash burn (expenses − revenue):
  - Implied gross operating expenses = revenue + net burn = $223,333 + $72,000 = $295,333 / month
  - Runway (net-burn basis) = cash / net burn = 5,200,000 / 72,000 ≈ 72.2 months
  - Runway (gross-expense basis) = cash / gross expenses = 5,200,000 / 295,333 ≈ 17.6 months (~18 months)
- If instead the stated 20 months runway is the figure you want to use:
  - Implied monthly burn used for that runway = 5,200,000 / 20 = $260,000
  - That would imply net burn = gross expenses − revenue = 260,000 − 223,333 = $36,667 net burn/month (which does not match the $72K figure)

Bottom line: the different runway numbers come from whether you present runway against net cash burn (very long runway here) or against total monthly cash out (conservative runway ~18–20 months). It’s normal to show both and clearly label which definition each uses.

2) Suggested finance narrative (ready for an internal deck)
Executive summary (1–2 lines)
- Metro Manufacturing is well capitalized with $5.2M cash on hand. On a net-cash-burn basis (current net burn ≈ $72K/month) we have the equivalent of ~6 years of runway; on a gross-operating-spend basis (monthly operating spend ≈ $295K) we have roughly 18 months of runway. We recommend using the conservative gross-spend runway (18–20 months) for planning and scenario analysis.

Current cash position & runway
- Cash: $5.2M.
- Trailing quarter revenue: $670K (≈ $223K/month). Annualized run rate ≈ $2.68M.
- Net cash burn: $72K/month (company is consuming $72K more cash than it generates each month).
- Implication: net-burn runway = ~72 months; gross-expense runway = ~18 months. For planning, present both and use the gross-expense runway as the base case.

Burn trend and drivers
- Revenue vs Spend: Revenue (~$223K/month) covers a significant portion of monthly operating cost; the remaining gap is the stated net burn of $72K/month.
- Key drivers of spend: (list actual drivers — e.g., R&D payroll, manufacturing ramp, inventory build, one-time capital investments, marketing spend). These items explain the delta between revenue and gross spend.
- Trend: If revenue growth continues at current rates and expenses remain constant, net burn will compress and cash runway will expand over time. Conversely, planned hiring, product investments, or inventory build will accelerate cash outflow and shorten runway.

Cash flow forecast & assumptions (what to call out)
- Forecast horizon: 12–24 months.
- Base-case assumptions: current monthly revenue = $223K; gross operating cost = $295K/month; net burn = $72K/month; no large one-off capital investments; minimal seasonality.
- Sensitivity scenarios: best case (revenue growth +10–20%/qtr), base case (flat revenue, current spend), downside (revenue -20% or a one-time spend of $500K).
- Show a 3-scenario runway table and 12–24 month cash-balance chart.

Risks, mitigants, and actions
- Risks: slower-than-expected revenue, inventory or receivable timing, hiring needs, one-time capital projects.
- Mitigants: tighten discretionary spend, delay non-critical hires, accelerate collections, consider staged inventory buys, or explore bridge financing if pursuing aggressive growth initiatives.
- Recommended immediate actions: (1) reconcile P&L & cash forecast monthly; (2) produce a 12-month rolling cash forecast and scenario dashboard; (3) decide which runway metric (net vs gross) is standard for internal reporting.

3) Suggested visuals for the deck
- One-line cash summary slide (cash, net burn, runway net & gross).
- Cash balance forecast (12–24 months) with three-scenario bands.
- Monthly waterfall chart: revenue → gross spend components → net burn for last 6–12 months (trend).
- Runway sensitivity table (cash / net burn; cash / gross monthly spend; scenarios).
- Key assumptions slide (revenue recognition timing, receivables, one-offs).

4) Quick example language you can paste into a slide or memo
Executive summary paragraph (short)
- “As of [date], Metro Manufacturing holds $5.2M in cash. Trailing-quarter revenue was $670K (≈ $223K/month). Our trailing net cash burn is $72K/month, which implies ~72 months of runway on a net-burn basis. A more conservative presentation comparing cash to total monthly operating spend (≈ $295K/month) gives a runway of ~18 months. For planning and stress-testing we recommend using the conservative 18–20 month runway and presenting net-burn runway as an upside scenario.”

5) Next steps I can help with
- Reconcile and produce the 12- and 24-month cash forecast (I’ll need monthly P&L lines or at minimum: monthly revenue, payroll, COGS, SG&A, capex, one-time items, and AR/AP timing).
- Create the slide-ready charts and the three-scenario sensitivity outputs.
- Draft a short CFO note for leadership summarizing the numbers for the board.

Which runway definition do you want to use in the materials (net-burn or gross-expense)? And do you want me to build the 12-month cash forecast and the scenario charts next — if so, please share monthly P&L or at least expected monthly expense breakdowns and any planned one-offs.

