Thanks — I can help. First, a quick check: one of your numbers doesn’t line up if we treat them as the usual definitions. I’ll show the math, then give a ready-to-use cash flow / burn trend narrative, suggested slides/visuals, and next steps.

Quick math and a noted inconsistency
- Cash on hand: $5,200,000
- Reported burn rate: $72,000 / month
- Reported runway: 20 months
- Revenue last quarter: $670,000 → ≈ $223,333 / month

Two interpretations:

1) If the burn rate $72K is the net burn (cash outflow after revenue offsets)
   - Runway = cash / net burn = 5,200,000 / 72,000 ≈ 72.2 months (~6 years)
   - This contradicts the stated 20 months.

2) If the runway of 20 months is correct, implied net burn = cash / runway = 5,200,000 / 20 = $260,000 / month
   - If monthly revenue is $223,333, then implied gross burn (total cash outflows before revenue) ≈ $260,000 + $223,333 = $483,333 / month.

3) Another possibility: $72K is a partial metric (e.g., payroll-only burn) rather than company net burn. If so, we need clarity on definitions.

Please confirm which of these is correct (is $72K net burn after revenue or something else?), or share the P&L/cash-flow for the last 3–6 months so I can compute trends precisely. Meanwhile, below are narratives and supporting materials you can drop directly into internal financial materials depending on the correct interpretation.

Executive summary language (pick the correct one)
A — If $72K is net burn (after revenue)
- "As of today we have $5.2M in cash and a net burn of ~$72K/month, which provides approximately 72 months (6 years) of runway at current operating levels. Revenue for the most recent quarter was $670K (≈$223K/month). With this cash position we are well-funded for the medium term, allowing us to prioritize strategic growth initiatives and selective investment in manufacturing capacity/automation. Key focus: maintain discipline on discretionary spend and monitor revenue seasonality to preserve optionality."

B — If runway = 20 months is correct (implied net burn ≈ $260K/month)
- "As of today we have $5.2M in cash and an implied net burn of ~$260K/month, which gives us ~20 months of runway at current rates. Last quarter revenue was $670K (≈$223K/month). To preserve runway and maintain flexibility we recommend focusing on margin improvement, prioritizing high-ROI capex, and reviewing hiring and vendor commitments. We will present 3 scenarios (base, downside, upside) and recommended triggers for cost action."

Key points to call out in the narrative
- Definitions: explicitly define gross burn (total cash outflows), revenue, and net burn (gross burn − revenue). State whether runway is calculated on net burn.
- Liquidity position: $5.2M cash — state any restrictions/earmarked amounts.
- Revenue context: $670K last quarter; indicate growth trend (QoQ/YoY) if available.
- Burn drivers: list primary components (payroll, raw materials inventory ramp, rent, capex, one-time expenses). If you don’t have the breakdown ready, call out top 3–5 cost drivers as placeholders to fill in.
- Scenario analysis: 12-month and 24-month projections under:
  - Base: current run-rate
  - Upside: +10–20% revenue growth, controlled cost
  - Downside: revenue flat/decline and slight increase in costs
  Provide months of runway and cash balance for each.

Suggested visuals/charts (use in slide deck)
- Cash runway chart: cash balance projection vs months under base/upside/downside scenarios.
- Monthly net burn trend (last 12 months): bars for gross burn, line for revenue, net burn as an overlay.
- Waterfall of change in cash vs prior period (show one-off receipts or payments).
- Revenue trend by month/quarter and gross margin trend.
- Cost breakdown pie (payroll, COGS, opex, capex).
- Sensitivity table: runway months vs changes in revenue and expense (-20% to +20%).

Sample 3–4 bullet summary for an internal slide (concise)
- “Cash: $5.2M. Last quarter revenue: $670K (≈$223K/month).”
- “Net burn: [confirm $72K / implied $260K] → runway: [~72 months / 20 months].”
- “Primary drivers of burn: payroll (~X%), raw materials/inventory (~Y%), and one-time ramp costs for equipment (~Z%).”
- “Recommendation: approve 12-month base plan; hold hiring for non-critical roles; run sensitivity scenarios and present triggers at $X remaining cash.”

Recommended action items / governance
- Reconcile and confirm the burn definition immediately (gross vs net).
- Produce a 12-month cash-flow forecast (weekly or monthly granularity).
- Produce 3 scenarios (base/upside/downside) and recommended trigger points (e.g., when runway < 12 months or cash < $2M).
- Review discretionary spend and hiring freeze thresholds tied to runway.
- Prepare a short board-ready memo summarizing the above.

Metrics to track weekly/monthly
- Net burn (monthly)
- Gross burn by category (payroll, materials, opex)
- Revenue vs forecast
- Cash balance and committed liabilities
- Receivable aging / inventory days

If you want, I can:
- Draft the exact slide wording for the executive summary and board memo (you can paste your slide template).
- Build the 12-month cash-flow projection and 3 scenarios — I’ll need monthly revenue and expense detail or prior 6–12 months P&L / cash flow (or at minimum the definitions of the $72K burn and whether runway 20 months is intended).

Next step
Tell me which of these is correct:
- $72K = net burn after revenue (so runway ≈ 72 months), or
- 20 months runway is the intended figure (so implied net burn ≈ $260K/month), or
- $72K refers to a subset (payroll-only or something else).

If you confirm, I’ll produce a polished finance narrative and the scenario tables/slides you can drop into your internal materials.

