A. Narrative if net burn = $72K/month (runway ≈ 72 months)
Executive summary
- Metro Manufacturing holds $5.2M in cash and is running a net cash burn of $72K/month, providing ~72 months of runway at current cadence. Revenue last quarter was $670K (≈ $223K/month), so the business is generating healthy top-line cash relative to spend.
What this means
- Strong liquidity position: multi-year runway gives time to invest in growth initiatives (R&D, sales expansion), de-risk product launches, and be selective about capital raises.
- Immediate focus can be on scaling revenue and strategic investments rather than urgent cash preservation.
Drivers & context
- Revenue of ~$223K/month covers a large portion of operating costs; net burn reflects discretionary investments or one-time items.
- Runway computed on current net burn (does not include planned increases in spend).
Risks & caveats
- A long runway can invite complacency: hiring or spending increases will shorten runway materially.
- Concentration risks (large customers, single product) or revenue volatility could change the story quickly.
Suggested leadership message (short)
- “With $5.2M on the balance sheet and a net burn of $72K/month, we have approximately six years of runway at current operations. This allows us to prioritize targeted investment in growth while maintaining financial flexibility. We will continue monthly cash forecasting and stress tests to ensure runway stays sufficient under multiple scenarios.”
Recommended asks / next steps
- Approve a 12‑month cash forecast that layers in planned hires and growth initiatives.
- Stress test scenarios where revenue dips 20% or planned spend increases 50% — quantify runway impact.
- Update board/leadership monthly with cash vs. plan and any committed spend that materially reduces runway.

